+**Nine forward migrations, all live-verified**:
+
+1. `inventory_cancel_movement` — added actor-identity (`28000`) +
+   permission (`42501`, surfaced as the non-leaking `P0002` "not found
+   or not accessible" message) checks; `anon`/`PUBLIC` EXECUTE revoked.
+   **CRITICAL**: pre-fix, this function was `SECURITY DEFINER`
+   (bypasses RLS), carried live `anon` EXECUTE, and had ZERO checks of
+   any kind — any fully unauthenticated caller could cancel any DRAFT
+   movement in ANY organization. Live-reproduced and closed.
+2. `inventory_prevent_header_modification`/`inventory_prevent_line_
+   modification` — the posted-header/line immutability triggers
+   redesigned. The session GUC (`ambra.inventory_movement_engine`) is
+   removed ENTIRELY as an authorization signal — a caller-settable GUC
+   was never a real security boundary (long-disclosed since before
+   IC-2). Authorization is now based on the SUBSTANCE of the change:
+   once posted, the ONLY value-changing transition ever permitted is
+   EXACTLY the accepted reversal lifecycle (`status: posted→reversed`,
+   `reversal_movement_id`/`reversed_by`/`reversed_at` from NULL to real
+   values, every other column byte-identical to OLD — verified via
+   `to_jsonb(NEW) - {allowed keys} = to_jsonb(OLD) - {allowed keys}`,
+   robust against future column additions), AND the new `reversal_
+   movement_id` must genuinely double-link back to a real row with
+   `original_movement_id = OLD.id` AND `movement_type_code = '900'`
+   (which cannot be manually created — IC-7A's own closed contract) —
+   closing the forgery surface completely (an attacker can no longer
+   even fake an "already reversed" state pointing at an arbitrary id).
+   Lines: no legitimate path ever updates/deletes an existing line
+   post-posting (verified against both `inventory_reverse_movement`'s
+   own body — only INSERTs new lines into a NEW reversal movement — and
+   `inventory_finalize_posting_internal`'s own body — the only line-
+   touching UPDATE happens while the header is still draft), so the
+   GUC-gated exception was removed entirely — unconditional denial.
+3. `inventory_guard_balance_write`/`inventory_guard_settings_write` —
+   fixed a SEPARATE, MORE SEVERE, newly-discovered systemic bug: both
+   used `current_setting('ambra.inventory_movement_engine', true) <>
+   'on'` as their own enforcement condition. When the GUC was NEVER SET
+   AT ALL (the ordinary default for any raw client connection — no
+   bypass trick required), `current_setting(..., true)` returns NULL;
+   `NULL <> 'on'` evaluates to NULL; PL/pgSQL's `IF NULL THEN` treats
+   NULL as FALSE — the guard failed OPEN, not closed. Live-reproduced:
+   a fresh, zero-GUC-contact transaction's raw UPDATE of `on_hand_
+   quantity` succeeded pre-fix. Fixed with `COALESCE(current_setting(
+   ...), 'off') <> 'on'`. Every legitimate canonical RPC already
+   explicitly sets this GUC before writing, so this changes zero
+   legitimate behavior.
+4. `inventory_save_draft` — same CRITICAL unauthenticated-exploit class
+   as item 1, same fix. `inventory_reconcile_balances` — a read-only
+   diagnostic carrying live `anon` EXECUTE with zero checks (cross-
+   tenant stock-drift read exposure); converted `LANGUAGE sql` →
+   `LANGUAGE plpgsql` solely to add the actor+permission check (the
+   diagnostic query itself unchanged); old 2-arg signature explicitly
+   dropped after confirming zero application callers.
+5. `inventory_reservations`/`_reservation_lines`/`inventory_
+   allocations`/`_allocation_lines`/`inventory_containers`/`_container_
+   lines` — new RESTRICTIVE `USING(false)/WITH CHECK(false)` policies
+   for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, the exact pattern IC-4/IC-5 already proved
+   safe. Closes IC-0's own 2026-09-15 finding (single PERMISSIVE ALL
+   policy, no ownership-aware RESTRICTIVE policy — re-confirmed still
+   live this phase via both `pg_policies` inspection and an empirical
+   raw-DML probe as a real `operate`-permissioned actor). Containers:
+   only the GENERIC (non-RepairOrder-owned) rows needed this closure —
+   confirmed safe only because IC-6 already deleted the sole legacy
+   direct-writer (`ambra-location-inventory.ts`'s own 4 write actions).
+6. `inventory_create_reservation`/`inventory_release_reservation`/
+   `inventory_create_allocation`/`inventory_release_allocation` —
+   **genuinely new finding, discovered live while applying migration
+   5**: all 4 were `SECURITY INVOKER` (the implicit Postgres default,
+   never explicitly set), out of compliance with this project's own
+   standing "SECURITY DEFINER, owner postgres" convention, and none had
+   an actor-identity check. Because INVOKER functions run AS the
+   calling role and are themselves subject to RLS, migration 5's own
+   new RESTRICTIVE policies correctly blocked ordinary raw client
+   writes but ALSO blocked these 4 RPCs' own legitimate writes (live-
+   reproduced: `42501: new row violates row-level security policy` on
